// Run-parity coverage for the CLR spill area's own WIDTH, not the
// multi-slot LOAD ORDER 35_folded_match_deficit_two_plus.lox already
// covers. computeAggregateNeeds (src/backend/clr_emitter.cpp) widens the
// shared scratch area for a `nativePops`-covered consumer whenever its
// fold deficit leaves two or more genuine operands still live above the
// fold — SLICE and SET_INDEX are both such consumers. Each shape below
// puts one of them in a chunk that builds NO aggregate at all: no CALL,
// INVOKE, SUPER_INVOKE, BUILD_LIST, or BUILD_MAP, so nothing else in the
// same chunk happens to size the spill area wide enough by coincidence.
//
// This file lives under clr-only/ for the same reason
// clr-only/35_folded_match_deficit_two_plus.lox does: the mechanism under
// test — one shared scratch area, sized once per chunk by scanning every
// instruction the shared abstract-stack analysis reports a fold deficit
// for — is a CLR-only concern (the JVM backend reorders with `swap`
// instead of a scratch area of a computed width), even though every shape
// below also happens to already run correctly on the JVM backend today.

// Shape 1: SLICE in a script chunk. The folded match result and its two
// slice bounds are the only operands SLICE ever reads, so nothing else
// in this chunk touches the spill area.
print (match 1 { case 1 => "abcdef" case _ => "xy" })[1:3];

// Shape 2: SET_INDEX in a FUNCTION chunk. A function's own Invoke method
// has its own, separately computed spill area — this shape catches a
// sizing pass that only widens the area correctly for a script chunk.
fun setit(xs) {
    (match 1 { case 1 => xs case _ => xs })[0] = 9;
}
var lst = [1, 2, 3];
setit(lst);
print lst;

// Shape 3: SLICE in a FUNCTION chunk, returned rather than printed
// directly — RETURN's own fold repair (bytecode-translation-problems.md)
// runs in the same chunk as this SLICE, so this shape also exercises two
// `nativePops`-covered consumers sharing one scratch area within a single
// function body.
fun slice(s) {
    return (match 1 { case 1 => s case _ => "" })[1:3];
}
print slice("abcdef");
